<ModellingLayout title="Names, choices and classification" description="A shared vocabulary does more than standardise spelling. It explains what a term means, which choices belong together and what an organising hierarchy does—and does not—claim." proseClass="prose wide modelling-document learning-document">
  <h2 id="layers">The thing, the concept and the words</h2>
  <p>Flat 1 at Harbour Court is a fictional dwelling. A defined concept used to classify it—perhaps “flat”—is not that dwelling. The text displayed for that concept is a label. Keeping these apart lets someone use familiar words without making the words responsible for deciding identity.</p>
  <p>A vocabulary can give one concept a preferred label and several alternative labels for the same meaning in its scope. That does not establish that “flat” and “apartment” are interchangeable everywhere. First compare the definitions and the intended use. If they differ materially, calling them synonyms conceals the question rather than resolving it.</p>

  <h2 id="term-specimen">Inspect a term, not just a word list</h2>
  <p>This specimen belongs to a fictional teaching vocabulary about physical access during inspections. It is not an approved OPDA value or a professional inspection standard.</p>
  <section class="learning-example document-flow" aria-labelledby="some-access">
    <h3 id="some-access">Concept: access to some but not all agreed areas</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>Preferred label</dt><dd>Access to some agreed areas.</dd>
      <dt>Alternative label</dt><dd>Partial physical access, used here only with this defined scope.</dd>
      <dt>Definition</dt><dd>During the identified visit, physical access was obtained to at least one, but not every, area in the nonempty set of identified agreed inspection areas.</dd>
      <dt>Example</dt><dd>The living room and bedroom were accessed; the third agreed area, an enclosed area, was not.</dd>
      <dt>Exclusions</dt><dd>Access to all agreed areas; access to none; permission to read or share a report.</dd>
      <dt>Teaching code</dt><dd>SOME. The code identifies this choice within this fictional list; its letters do not define the meaning.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <p>The definition does work the label cannot do alone. “Partial” might otherwise mean part of a day, some documents or limited visibility within every area. The example shows an ordinary case; the exclusions block plausible misunderstandings. A code helps exchange the choice, but an inherited code such as “P” could mean something else in another source.</p>
  <p>A preferred-label edit from “Access to some agreed areas” to “Some agreed areas accessed” could leave the meaning unchanged. Extending the definition to include access to none would change meaning, even if the code and displayed label stayed the same. Review the definition, not just the text diff.</p>

  <h2 id="choices">A choice list needs a scope</h2>
  <p>For this lesson, the question is precisely: <em>which proportion of a nonempty, identified set of agreed areas was physically accessed during one identified visit?</em> Each area either was or was not accessed under the exercise's stated convention. We have complete evidence for those outcomes when selecting a choice.</p>
  <div class="learning-table">
    <table>
      <caption>Three choices for the fictional physical-access question</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Choice</th><th scope="col">Definition</th><th scope="col">Example with three agreed areas</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">All</th><td>Every agreed area was accessed.</td><td>Three accessed.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Some</th><td>At least one, but not every, agreed area was accessed.</td><td>One or two accessed.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">None</th><td>No agreed area was accessed.</td><td>Zero accessed.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Under these premises the choices do not overlap, and they cover every known access outcome. The nonempty scope matters: with no agreed areas, ordinary “all” and “none” wording becomes misleading. Unknown access evidence is a different problem. It cannot be converted into “None” simply because no access statement arrived.</p>
  <p>A closed choice list means a particular use permits only its defined choices until the list is deliberately revised. It does not mean the world contains only the three inspection events already on a screen. Newly identified dwellings and inspections can join an open population without becoming new vocabulary values.</p>

  <LearningDiagram kind="vocabulary" caption="Names, scoped choices, topic organisation and independent facets answer different questions. The all/some/none access choices rely on a nonempty identified inspection scope." />

  <h2 id="hierarchy">A topic tree is not a diagram of a building's parts</h2>
  <section class="learning-example document-flow" aria-labelledby="topics">
    <h3 id="topics">A fictional report-topic taxonomy</h3>
    <ul><li>Building fabric<ul><li>Roof condition</li><li>Wall condition</li></ul></li></ul>
    <p>“Roof condition” is a narrower topic beneath “Building fabric”. This organises what reports discuss. It does not identify a roof, make a report a kind of building or state that a roof-condition concept is a physical part.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="learning-example document-flow" aria-labelledby="facets">
    <h3 id="facets">Independent ways to describe a report</h3>
    <dl><dt>Topic</dt><dd>Roof condition.</dd><dt>Document status</dt><dd>Draft, under this example's document convention.</dd><dt>Information type</dt><dd>Inspection account.</dd></dl>
    <p>The same report can carry all three classifications. “Draft” need not sit beneath “Roof condition” in a single tree. Each facet asks a different question.</p>
  </section>
  <p>A broader/narrower topic relationship helps someone browse from a general subject to a more specific one. A kind-of relationship makes a different claim about what things are. A physical part-of relationship makes another. One drawing convention should not let those meanings slide into each other unnoticed.</p>

  <section class="learning-example document-flow" aria-labelledby="vocabulary-practice">
    <h2 id="vocabulary-practice">Practice: repair meaning and scope</h2>
    <p>A proposed definition says “Partial access means access that was partial”. Its choices are “All agreed areas accessed”, “Limited access” and “Not completed”. A report-topic tree places “Draft report” beneath “Roof condition”. What needs attention?</p>
    <h3>Worked response</h3>
    <p>The circular definition does not tell a reader what counts. The earlier “at least one, but not every, identified agreed area” definition provides an assessable boundary for this teaching scope. Test it with two of three areas accessed, and with zero of three.</p>
    <p>The proposed list mixes access with completion. An inspection could be completed within its agreed reporting scope while physical access was limited. “Not completed” could also overlap “Limited access”. Separate the questions before choosing labels, then test the access choices against the nonempty area set.</p>
    <p>“Draft report” expresses status, not a narrower roof-condition topic. Keep it as an independent facet. A report can discuss roof condition while draft or issued; the subject organisation should not force one status.</p>
    <Callout title="Does adding “Other” solve the problem?" tone="important" heading="h3">
      <p>Not here. Under the complete, nonempty all/some/none premises, every known outcome already has a place. An “Other” choice would need a new, explained meaning or a changed scope. It would not supply missing evidence. In a different list that deliberately leaves cases open, “Other” can indicate a known case outside the named choices—but it still differs from “unknown”.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <h2 id="connections">Correspondence is more than matching labels</h2>
  <p>A recipient might define “restricted access” to include both some access and no access. Our “Some” choice then has a narrower meaning, not merely a less confident exact match. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/explore/contexts-and-connections#mapping-example">worked correspondence</a> compares the definitions and demonstrates the information lost in substitution.</p>
  <p>A useful vocabulary review supplies an ordinary case, an exclusion and the question the choice must answer. It need not propose the code or technical notation. For a complete applied exercise, use <a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/review-vocabularies-and-mappings">Review choices and mappings</a>.</p>
  <p id="source-contract" class="learning-note">The <a href="/glossary#mapping">canonical glossary</a> and <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/standards-and-decisions">method decision register</a> remain shared references. These teaching specimens are not another maintained candidate vocabulary.</p>
</ModellingLayout>
